TODO
----

A list of open questions / possible improvements / ... related to the
hashjoin bloom filter pushdown. In arbitrary order.


* properly sizing the Bloom filter

  Sometimes it's better to use a cheaper less accurate filter, smaller
  one and/or with fewer hash functions. It's a trade off between false
  positive rate and cheaper probes (e.g. when it fits into CPU cache).
  We probably need a simple cost model, or some heuristics. We should
  also reflect this in the path costing.


* cost false positive rate

  Right now we assume the filter makes no errors, but that is not quite
  corret - the filter will accept some fraction of extra tuples, and
  those will be discarded by the join realizing the filter. If we start
  building filters with different false positive rates (e.g. to fit them
  into L2), it'd matter more.

  I'm not sure how to cost the false positive rate, because it depends
  on what happens between the scan and the realizing join - there may
  be other joins, and each "extra" tuple will have to pass through
  those too. But we don't know the details at the scan level, so we
  probably need some simple heuristic.


* stabilizing the join order

  Pushing down filters, makes the order of the related joins mostly
  irrelevant, and it can easily change based on tiny estimate changes.
  That should be mostly harmless (the order really should not matter),
  but it's a bit annoying for testing (random plan changes). Maybe we
  should add a bit of hysteresis, to prevent this?

  Maybe if we correctly consider the false positive rate, it would have
  such effect? 


* supporting other kinds of runtime filters

  Right now we only have "Bloom" filters, but there are more kinds of
  interesting filters. Other databases seem to support minmax and list
  filters, or possibly various hybrids (eg. starting as list, then
  morphing to Bloom/minmax). We should make it extensible this way.

  The other interesting option is to build per-attribute filters even
  for multi-column joins.

  We probably need an "abstract" filter API, implemented by the various
  filter kinds. With callbacks for adding entries, probing for a hash,
  estimating false positive rate, etc.

  We need to be careful about selectivity estimates, though. For example,
  a Bloom filter and minmax will have different selectivities, and the
  same thing applies to per-attribute filters (which will have a worse
  selectivity than the combined filter).


* do we need to add something for JIT to work?

  AFAIK we need to add some opcodes and handle them.


* parallel query support

  Right now we support only serial query, and parallel queries where
  the filter is built and consumed inside a worker (for a hashjoin that
  is not parallel-aware). It should be possible to support parallel
  queries too, with filters merged from workers. One of the earlier
  patch versions implemented that, actually.

  Another option is a filter built by the leader (in a serial hashjoin),
  and pushed down to the workers throught the Gather. This filter still
  needs to be copied into a chunk of shared memory.


* filter portability

  If we want to allow pushing filters into "external" executor nodes,
  we probably need a portable variant, not tied to our implementation
  details. For example, imagine pushing down a Bloom filter into a
  Custom Scan reading a Parquet file. Our Bloom filter is built using
  a custom hash function, both for calculating the initial hash value
  (from our the data) and then for calculating the filter probes. The
  Parquet file may contain a Bloom filter too, but it's not compatible
  with "our" filter - it's using xxHash, etc. It can still call our
  built-in hash function, but that may be inefficient, and maybe we
  should allow using a "more compatible" filter?

  In some advanced cases it may be actually hard to call our built-in
  hash functions - when a filter pushed outside Postgres. Consider for
  example a scan that pushes the filter into storage, or a foreign scan
  pushing the filter to a remote non-Postgres database. To support such
  cases we'd need a portable filter.

  It may be tricky to do, though. A data type may allow multiple binary
  representations for the same value - all of those must have the same
  hash. Similarly, our hashes are cross-type safe (so e.g. int2 vs. int4
  vs. int8 produce the same hash for the "same" value). A simple hash
  of the bytes won't do. But maybe we could restrict this to some "safe"
  subset of cases (no cross-type conditions, simple byval types).

  The minmax types may have similar issues, as it relies on collations
  (because of ordering). Both ends need to use exactly the same ordering
  rules. Ofc, for filters on integers this should not be an issue.

  BTW the Parquet docs might be a good guidance for sizing our filters:
  https://parquet.apache.org/docs/file-format/bloomfilter/


* non-scan filters

  Right now we push filters only to scans, but maybe we could also push
  them to intermediate nodes, e.g. on top of joins etc. This would help
  cases where the join clause is complex (references more than 2 rels),
  or when the node does not allow pushdown (e.g. because of outer join).

  AFAIK we already have some basic building blocks needed for this, like
  the generation of build relids sets. We'd need to add logic to
  consider filters when constructing paths for a join relation too,
  similarly to what we do for scan paths.

  Seems more like a possible future improvement.
